Let \(E\) be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space, let \(K\subseteq E\) be a nonempty closed convex cone, and let \(S=K\cap\{x:Ax=b\}
eq\varnothing\). Define \(F_{\min}\) as the intersection of all faces of \(K\) containing \(S\). Then \(F_{\min}\) is a face of \(K\), \(S\subseteq F_{\min}\), and
\[S\cap\operatorname{ri}F_{\min}
eq\varnothing.\]
Moreover, if a face \(F\unlhd K\) satisfies \(S\cap\operatorname{ri}F
eq\varnothing\), then \(F=F_{\min}\). Thus replacing \(K\) by \(F_{\min}\) preserves feasibility and restores relative strict feasibility.

Let scenarios have probabilities \(p_s>0\), local variables \(x_s\), and consensus constraints \(x_s- ar x=0\) with multipliers \(
u_s\). For an extensive-form objective \(g( ar x)+\sum_{s\in S}p_sf_s(x_s,y_s)\), scenario-multiplier consistency requires every local stationarity equation to use the same probability convention and the consensus-variable balance \(0\in\partial g( ar x)-\sum_{s\in S}
u_s+N_X( ar x)\). If \(g=0\) and \(X\) is the whole ambient space, this reduces to \(\sum_{s\in S}
u_s=0\). Multipliers stored without probability weights must be converted consistently before this balance is tested.
